<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Séminaire de Québec</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1663">1663</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Séminaire de Québec is a Catholic seminary founded in 1663 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francois-de-laval">François de Laval</a>, first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a>, to train priests for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a>. It became the centre of the colonial church and of education in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, and in 1852 it founded Université Laval, the first French-language university in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/seminaire-de-quebec.jpg" alt="Séminaire de Québec" width="320" height="226" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gilbert Bochenek · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qu%C3%A9bec-Grand-S%C3%A9minaire-1.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Bishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francois-de-laval">François de Laval</a> founded the seminary on 26 March 1663 to sustain the mission of the Church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, and in 1665 he joined it to the Seminary of Foreign Missions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> as the Seminary of Foreign Missions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a>, the source of the initials SME still in use. Its first task was to prepare young men for parishes and missions as far away as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a>. Laval's bequest of a large tract of lakes and forests north-east of the city, the Beaupré Seigneury, bought from the Compagnie des 100 Associés, has funded its work ever since.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1668 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-baptiste-colbert">Jean-Baptiste Colbert</a>, chief minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>, began an attempt to impose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-language">French language</a> and culture on the indigenous peoples, and Laval opened the seminary to indigenous children and to settlers' children who wished to become priests. This was the beginning of the Petit Séminaire, the minor seminary. Until the conquest of 1760 it was a boarding school whose pupils attended classes at the Jesuit College; when the Jesuits were suppressed after the conquest, the seminary's directors took over and the Petit Séminaire became a college open to all boys.</p><p>Many French-Canadian clergy and academics of the 18th and 19th centuries passed through it. Its teaching was recognised in a royal charter from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a> in 1852, which led to Université Laval. Until 1970 the Superior of the seminary was also the university's Rector; the university became a separate corporation that year, and the Petit Séminaire became a private Catholic secondary school, separate from the seminary, in 1987.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The seminary's buildings in Old Quebec range from the 17th to the 20th century: the Vieux-Séminaire, built on the model of 17th-century French colleges, and later buildings such as the Camille-Roy and Jean-Olivier-Briand buildings, the second housing the priests' residence and the Grand Séminaire. The site at 1 Rempart Street was designated a National Historic Site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> in 1929, and part of the Vieux-Séminaire has housed the university's school of architecture since 1987. The seminary still trains priests and other pastoral leaders. It was the subject of Léonard Forest's 1964 documentary Walls of Memory.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Séminaire de Québec” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The bishop of Quebec, beginning with François de Laval, vicar apostolic from 1658 and bishop from 1674, headed a powerful church that also founded the <b>Séminaire de Québec</b> in 1663.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-france">New France · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
